#{[17592186560198 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x + 2 y)\\,i+(x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{4 \\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [17592186560215 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x y^{2})\\,i+(2 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(e^{t})\\,i+(\\sqrt{t})\\,j$ for $t\\in [1,3]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [17592186560219 :question/problem "Which integral is obtained from $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x + 4 y)\\,i+(2 x + 3 y)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(t \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(t \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi}{3}]$? Use the supplied parameterization and limits to compute $F(r(t))\\cdot r'(t)$. Do not evaluate the integral or change variables."] [17592186560971 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ may be written as a composition of Jordan blocks, as follows:\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-3)\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n- a single $2\\times 2$ Jordan block corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ and\n\n- a single $1\\times 1$ block corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chains: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{2}(1) & : & {v}_{2}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }{v}_{1}\\overset{A-I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {J}_{1}(-3) & : & {v}_{3}\\overset{A+3I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that \n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1,$\n\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector ${v}_{2}$ such that $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}.$\n\nNow, computing $A-I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation $(A-I){v}_{2}={v}_{1}$ is equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 7 & 11 & -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-7){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & -4 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we have ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}+2.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+({x}_{3}+2)=-1\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-{x}_{3}-3.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3}-3 \\\\ {x}_{3}+2 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=0,$ we get  \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $b-c=2-0=2.$"] [17592186560984 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y}-y,\\,x{e}^{y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨{e}^{y},\\,0⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{5}+{x}^{7},\\,\\frac{6x}{5}⟩$"] [17592186560990 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$"] [17592186560990 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$  is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since all entries are zero.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the main diagonal are not the same.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\""] [17592186560992 :question/problem "Which of the following are rotation-scaling matrices?\n\nI. $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\n\nII. $B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nIII. $C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$"] [17592186560992 :question/worked-solution "A rotation-scaling matrix has the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers that are not equal to zero simultaneously. Notice that the entries on the main diagonal are the same, while the antidiagonal entries have opposite signs.\n\nWith that in mind let's check each of the given matrices:\n\n- $A=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 9 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $B=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]$ is a rotation-scaling matrix.\n\n- $C=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 9\\end{aligned}]$ is *not* a rotation-scaling matrix since the entries on the antidiagonal do not have opposite signs.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186560997 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-35 \\\\ -25\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$"] [17592186560998 :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda =7,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies \n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-7I)v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-7I)^{2}v=0.$ First, we have\n\n$$\nA-7I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nComputing its square, we see that this matrix is nilpotent:\n\n$$\n(A-7I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{2}$ is a solution to $(A-7I)^{2}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-7I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-7I)v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\nA-7I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nConverting this matrix to reduced row echelon form, we get\n\n$$\nA-7I∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means that the solution to $(A-7I)v=0$ is ${v}_{2}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ is a free variable. Therefore, the solution to $(A-7I)v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing our two solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-7I)^{2}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-7I)v=0.$  \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ of $A.$"] [17592186561030 :question/worked-solution "Given a matrix $A$ with eigenvalue $\\lambda ,$ a generalized eigenvector of rank $k$ is a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)^{k}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-\\lambda I)^{k-1}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nTo find a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ corresponding to $\\lambda =1,$ we need to find a vector $v$ that satisfies\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{3}v=0\\,\\text{and}\\,(A-I)^{2}v\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each condition:\n\n- We start by solving $(A-I)^{3}v=0.$ We're given that\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis means that *any* vector in ${R}^{3}$ is a solution to $(A-I)^{3}v=0.$ Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)^{3}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ We have\n\n$$\n(A-I)^{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,.\n$$\n\nThis matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. This means that the solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ is ${v}_{3}=0,$ where ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are free variables. Therefore, the solution to $(A-I)^{2}v=0$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ {v}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} \\\\ {v}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]={v}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{v}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the solutions, we see that the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ satisfies $(A-I)^{3}v=0$ but does *not* satisfy $(A-I)^{2}v=0.$ \n\nHence, $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $3$ of $A.$"] [17592186561040 :question/problem "Find ${e}^{At}$ for $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].$ \n\n*Hint: The only eigenvalue of $A$ is $2,$ and it has multiplicity two.*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${e}^{At}={e}^{2t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$4t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-4t$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186561057 :question/problem "A linear transformation $T$ represents a dilation.  Given that $v=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]$ and $T(v)=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],$ what is the standard matrix of $T?$"] [17592186561104 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=2 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,2),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561108 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561110 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(2,12 \\pi),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(\\cos{\\left(\\frac{y}{x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,dy=$ The composed integrand at the initial endpoint is interpreted by its one-sided continuous extension along $C.$"] [17592186561117 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 x\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561123 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2} - 3$ traversed from the point $A(0,-3)$ to point $B(2,1),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(x^{2} y\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 x^{2} \\left(y + 3\\right)\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561136 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,3),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(3 y\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561139 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(0,0)$ to point $B(1,1),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(3 x\\right)\\,dx+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561153 :question/problem "If the path $C$ is a part of the graph $y=3 \\pi x^{2}$ traversed from the point $A(1,3 \\pi)$ to point $B(3,27 \\pi),$ then ${\\int }_C\\left(\\sin{\\left(\\frac{y}{2 x^{2}} \\right)}\\right)\\,dx+\\left(x\\right)\\,dy=$"] [17592186561273 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186561290 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{12}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186561292 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{2 \\pi}{3}} \\int_{2}^{4} r^{3} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186561297 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{8}} \\int_{0}^{4 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186561303 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{\\sin{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 2 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186561304 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\int_{0}^{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} \\int_{0}^{2 \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}} 3 r\\,\\mathrm d r\\,\\mathrm d \\theta.$"] [17592186561531 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩$"] [17592186561531 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186561533 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,9 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{2 x},\\,x - 3 e^{2 x}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + 2 e^{2 x + 2 y}⟩$"] [17592186561533 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(9 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{2 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{2 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{2 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{2 x}\\right)=1 - 9 e^{2 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(4 e^{2 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186561538 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{6} + 3 e^{3 x + 2 y},\\,\\frac{7 x}{6} + 2 e^{3 x + 2 y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨y^{2} - \\frac{y}{6},\\,2 x y + \\frac{5 x}{6}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨y e^{3 x},\\,x - e^{3 x}⟩$"] [17592186561538 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y} + \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)+\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(6 e^{3 x + 2 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 y - \\frac{1}{6}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2 y + \\frac{5}{6},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(2 y + \\frac{5}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y - \\frac{1}{6}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{6}\\right)+\\left(2 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{6}\\right)-\\left(2 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 3 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 3 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 4 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186561545 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{8} + 3 e^{3 x + y},\\,\\frac{9 x}{8} + e^{3 x + y}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y^{3} - \\frac{y}{8},\\,6 x y^{2} + \\frac{7 x}{8}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨2 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 2 e^{3 x}⟩$"] [17592186561545 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{9}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y} + \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{9}{8}\\right)+\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x + y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y^{2} + \\frac{7}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2} - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)+\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{8}\\right)-\\left(6 y^{2}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 6 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 6 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(2 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 8 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186561555 :question/problem "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\nI. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y^{2} - \\frac{y}{4},\\,6 x y + \\frac{3 x}{4}⟩$\n\nII. $F(x,y)=⟨\\frac{y}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y},\\,\\frac{5 x}{4} + 3 e^{3 x + 3 y}⟩$\n\nIII. $F(x,y)=⟨3 y e^{3 x},\\,x - 3 e^{3 x}⟩$"] [17592186561555 :question/worked-solution "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I gives the required area. In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6 y - \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=6 y + \\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(6 y + \\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y - \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(6 y\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(6 y\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{5}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y} + \\frac{1}{4}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{5}{4}\\right)+\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(9 e^{3 x + 3 y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3 e^{3 x}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1 - 9 e^{3 x},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1 - 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=\\left(1\\right)+\\left(- 9 e^{3 x}\\right)-\\left(3 e^{3 x}\\right)=1 - 12 e^{3 x}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186562465 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=-1$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=3.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1+3\\text{i}=-1+3\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+3\\text{i} \\\\ -1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =-1\\pm 3\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}1\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\pm 4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186562502 :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{200\\times 800}(R),$ the vector space of all $200\\times 800$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. ${R}_{500}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $500$\n\nIII. ${L}^{1}([-1,1]),$ the vector space of all functions  $f:[-1,1]\\to R$ such that ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}|f(x)|\\,\\text{d}x<\\infty $"] [17592186562503 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=2x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-2y+4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x(1-x)-xy)=2-4x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y+4xy)=4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y+4xy)=-2+4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}2-4x-y & -x \\\\ 4y & -2+4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}2-4(0.5)-(1) & -(0.5) \\\\ 4(1) & -2+4(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.5) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.5\\pm 0.5\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is $\\text{an asymptotically stable}$ $\\text{spiral sink}.$"] [17592186562511 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\n- From the matrix $C=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ -(-2) & 4\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that $\\text{Re}(\\lambda )=4$ and $\\text{Im}(\\lambda )=-2.$ So, our eigenvalue is\n\n$$\n\\lambda =4+(-2)\\text{i}=4-2\\text{i}.\n$$\n\n- From the matrix $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we deduce that our corresponding eigenvector is\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4+(-1)\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2+3\\text{i} \\\\ -4-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the second eigenvalue of $A$ must be the complex conjugate of $\\lambda ,$ and the second eigenvector must be the complex conjugate of $v.$\n\nTherefore, our eigenvalues and eigenvectors are \n\n$$\n\\lambda =4∓2\\text{i},\\,v=[\\begin{aligned}2\\pm 3\\text{i} \\\\ -4∓\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] [17592186562768 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-4x^2y)\\,dx+(y+4xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 2,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [17592186562773 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 1,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [17592186562783 :question/problem "![](images/5d/5def443330d0f3793a305101ad63ea19d8a6e0a7835a23018063bd6fb57d23a8.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(\\sqrt{5+x}-5x^2y)\\,dx+(e^{-y^2}+5xy^2)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above."] [17592186562793 :question/problem "![](images/9f/9f98c6d615262205474643c73e3d4b5bd8d94a8560d0ffbda34ad7aca362d1bd.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\oint_C(\\sqrt{x+1}-x^2y)\\,dx+(e^y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where the path $C$ is shown above."] [17592186562801 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-x^2y)\\,dx+(y+xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [17592186562805 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 3,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [17592186562808 :question/problem "Use Green's theorem to evaluate $\\oint_C(e^{-x^2}-3x^2y)\\,dx+(y+3xy^2)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the counterclockwise boundary of the disk with polar description $0\\le r\\le 4,\\ 0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi$."] [17592186563648 :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-1} \\\\ \\sqrt{t} \\\\ t+3\\end{aligned}]$  is continuous."] [17592186563651 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{16-(3x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186563662 :question/problem "Find the limit ${lim}_{t\\to 1}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=\\frac{4+t}{5}\\,i+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)}{{t}^{2}-1}\\,j+\\sqrt{9t}\\,k.$"] [17592186563667 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ (1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186563667 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\sqrt{s+8} & =\\sqrt{0+8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{3}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s}{s} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡s)}^{′}}{{s}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}}}{1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{s}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}(1+2s)^{1/(2s)} & ={lim}_{u\\to 0}(1+u)^{1/u}=e\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 1 \\\\ e\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186563668 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{|x-5|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-10x+27}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186563693 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖,$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186563693 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{1-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563699 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{15}{|5x+10|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+4x+5}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186563702 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-4x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(1) \\\\ y(1)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186563702 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4(t-1))\\, \\\\ \\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4(t-1))\\,\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563704 :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $16$ | $9$ | $12$ | $10$ | $11$ | $11$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $10$ | $14$ | $8$ | $10$ | $7$ | $8$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$"] [17592186563704 :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =16+9+12+10+11+11+6+9=84, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =10+14+8+10+7+8+2+4=63\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=695.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=695-\\frac{1}{8}(84)(63)=695-661.5=33.5.\n$$"] [17592186563711 :question/problem "Determine the interval on which the function $f(t)=\\frac{{t}^{2}+4t}{t}\\,i+({t}^{2}-1)\\,j+\\frac{1}{t-2}\\,k$ is continuous."] [17592186563712 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖$, if $f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-3t \\\\ \\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ 5-2t\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186563712 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} \\\\ {lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}({t}^{2}-3t) & ={3}^{2}-3(3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-8} & =\\frac{1}{{3}^{2}-8}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}(5-2t) & =5-2(3)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(t)‖$ as $t$ approaches $3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 3}‖f(t)‖ & =‖{lim}_{t\\to 3}f(t)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563716 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\,.$"] [17592186563716 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s}\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]\\,.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186563720 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{(x+2)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-4x}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186563744 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{3}{(x+3)\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}-6x}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186563754 :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s),$ if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ \\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ (1+5s)^{1-s}\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186563754 :question/worked-solution "First, recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1/(2s)}.\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one by one.\n\n- For the first component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\sqrt{5-s} & =\\sqrt{5-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the second component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\frac{{e}^{1-s}}{s}=\\frac{{e}^{1-1}}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the third component, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 1}\\,(1+5s)^{1-s} & =(1+5\\cdot 1)^{1-1} \\\\  & ={6}^{0} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 1}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186563755 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-2x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*"] [17592186563755 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) & -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$"] [17592186563824 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{6}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-6{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=36$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [17592186563828 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨3{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-2{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [17592186563837 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{1}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=1$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [17592186563840 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{2}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨4{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-3{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=4$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [17592186563841 :question/problem "Given that ${∮}_C F\\cdot dr=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{5}\\boxed{}\\,dr\\,d\\theta$, where $F(x,y)=⟨6{y}^{3}+{e}^{x},\\,\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}+10}-{x}^{3}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^2+y^2=25$ oriented counterclockwise, what expression belongs in the blank after applying Green’s theorem and changing to polar coordinates, simplified pointwise before integration?"] [17592186563860 :question/problem "Given that\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr = \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}\\boxed{}drdθ$,\nwhere $F(x, y) = ⟨5xy^{2}, 5x + e^{\\cos y}⟩$ and $C$ is the circle $x^{2} + y^{2} = 9$ oriented counterclockwise, what is the missing part of the equation? Use $x=r\\cos\\theta$, $y=r\\sin\\theta$, and simplify the integrand pointwise before integration."] [17592186563860 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can rewrite our integral as $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\oint_C(5 x y^{2})\\,dx+(5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}})\\,dy$.\n\nGreen's theorem states that $\\oint_C P\\,dx+Q\\,dy=\\iint_D(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y})\\,dA$.\n\nIn our case, the region $D$ and its boundary $C$ satisfy the conditions of Green's theorem:\n\n![](images/3b/3b1a8cf50ccf8ab3a73f31753def3c2fee2143a7372309cccf2ff7a5e68aedd9.png)\n\nSince $P(x,y)=5 x y^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=5 x + e^{\\cos{(y )}}$, we obtain $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=10 x y$, $\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=5$.\n\nApplying Green's theorem, we get $\\oint_C F\\cdot dr=\\iint_D(5-(10 x y))\\,dA=\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA$.\n\nNow, notice that the region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as $\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta):0\\le r\\le 3,0\\le\\theta\\le2\\pi\\}$.\n\nThe change of variables formula for plane polar coordinates states that $\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta$.\n\nTherefore, we can write our double integral as $\\iint_D(- 10 x y + 5)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta(- 10 r^{2} \\sin{(\\theta )} \\cos{(\\theta )} + 5)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta=\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^{3}r (- 5 r^{2} \\sin{(2 \\theta )} + 5)\\,dr\\,d\\theta$."] [17592186563918 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{x}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=8 y^{4} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}+4 y \\arctan{\\left(x \\right)}- \\frac{y^{2}}{x^{2}}.$"] [17592186563939 :question/problem "Find ${f}_{y}(x,y)$ for $f(x,y)=8 x^{4} \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}+9 x \\arctan{\\left(y \\right)}- \\frac{3 x^{2}}{y^{3}}.$"] [17592186563954 :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion  of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false, while statement ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ is true. Since the force $F$ acts perpendicular to the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B,$ it neither helps nor hinders its motion.\n\n![](images/dc/dcc2f364085ccca85e6e6ddd8a5affac51c84f774281b817d4852bd2322bcb1b.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$.\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$ is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ the force is still perpendicular to the motion.\n\n![](images/50/508e0ac5f67d7b119ad689ccaede8894268eefe31d9b00f148ddd6bf851de6b4.png)\n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$ only."] [17592186564480 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-{t}^{2}+3t-2 \\\\ -4t+8\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}-t+1 \\\\ 2t-3\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186564481 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$"] [17592186564485 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 1-x\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $x+y=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 1+0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+y & =1 \\\\ (1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [17592186564493 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ -3x+4\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $y=-3x+4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (0) & =-3(0)+4 \\\\ 0 & =4\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example,$a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ 4+1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-3x+4 \\\\ (5) & =-3(1)+4 \\\\ 5 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is true. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"IV only.\""] [17592186564495 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & \\frac{x}{y}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{x}{y}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0 \\\\ \\frac{x}{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡y=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\n \nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{1}=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is an stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))={0}^{2}-1\\cdot 1-=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,1)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,1).$"] [17592186564499 :question/problem "Let\n$T=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nbe the standard matrix of the linear transformation $T.$ Then $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto"] [17592186564506 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$\n\nIII. The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum of $f$"] [17592186564506 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we obtain   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})={({f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}))}_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})<0,$ the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186564519 :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $L$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2x-3\\end{aligned}],\\,x\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement I is false. The coordinates of the zero-vector do not satisfy the equation $2x-y=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(0)-(0) & =3 \\\\ 0 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Let, for example, $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ from $L.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0+1 \\\\ -3-1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nHowever, $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\notin L$ since substituting the coordinates into the equation, we obtain the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y & =3 \\\\ 2(1)-(-4) & =3 \\\\ 6 & =3\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $0\\notin L,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since statements II and III are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""] [17592186564529 :question/problem "The set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is linearly dependent. Which of the following vectors could be ${a}_{2}$ if\n${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]?$"] [17592186564531 :question/worked-solution "The least-squares solution $Ax=b$ satisfies the corresponding normal equation:\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}Ax={A}^{T}b\n$$\n\nSubstituting $A=QR,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}Ax & ={A}^{T}b \\\\ (QR)^{T}QRx & =(QR)^{T}b \\\\ {R}^{T}({Q}^{T}Q)Rx & ={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Q$ has orthonormal columns, ${Q}^{T}Q=I,$ and we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{T}Rx={R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${R}^{T}$ is invertible, pre-multiplying by $({R}^{T})^{-1}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}Rx & =({R}^{T})^{-1}\\cdot {R}^{T}{Q}^{T}b \\\\ Rx & ={Q}^{T}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting our matrices, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{R}x & =\\overbrace{\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 8\\end{aligned}]}^{{Q}^{T}}\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{b} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]x & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=-9 \\\\ {x}_{2}=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=9.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}_{1}+8(9) & =-9 \\\\ 9{x}_{1} & =-81 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the least-squares solution to $Ax=b$ is $[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186564532 :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the missing coefficients in the solution to the initial value problem, shown above.\n\n$x(t)=$ \n{{field-1}} \n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,\\cdot \\,[\\begin{aligned}t{e}^{2t} \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-t}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$"] [17592186564617 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"] [17592186564650 :question/worked-solution "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=1,\\,\\theta ={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$"] [17592186564664 :question/problem "![](images/42/42e881f2fea91ee9191c5b32dce49f485977ded98a9e900bfcaf51fc1e0f8ec1.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564664 :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/49/494158fe124fe1c8e1f7f618695dc9846991bd074b199a45a5874ddb8ad16e86.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{34}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{34}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{17 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{34 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{17}{3}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"] [17592186564668 :question/problem "![](images/00/00c1e8199146ddfdc1d8243eccfc5d5f44f9e67474722365a5c39c4a2226dcab.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564670 :question/problem "![](images/69/69ff60a180694c898c1ae208d6e2dd95ef82ec0f27b7e4c1cc0256a04ae696b9.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is a path that traverses a semicircle of radius $6$ once in the counterclockwise direction, as shown above."] [17592186564672 :question/problem "![](images/2a/2ae1eeb848f8d01597ec509c095d8a0307805640690d9d65102909e2b861e5d2.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564672 :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1e/1ec516fa1aa0172ba521d53fc11abd6268cac8eda4d1f221b741d5d9049dda99.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{13}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{13 \\pi}{6} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{13}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{13}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{13}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{13 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{13 \\pi}{6}+\\frac{13 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{13}{6}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"] [17592186564673 :question/problem "![](images/1a/1aad44e8db3ab56b858a003aada2ea280b879365de13842d7a41f9d7f89e822f.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564679 :question/problem "![](images/e0/e028416a02722d1d832c2daddddbb23a314694ddd328670ed58376681dc7c023.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564679 :question/worked-solution "The path $C$ is made up of two parts, as shown below:\n\n![](images/5d/5d932a90da98448134bdb2deb9b524636ef7ad9aa59a7e91f9fe0d75178f395e.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above.\n\nNow, we make use of the following facts:\n\n- If $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}$ is a piecewise-smooth curve, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- The line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$ is equal to the arc length $L$ of $C,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=L.\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is a quarter of a circle of radius $r=\\frac{16}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{1}{4}(2\\pi r)=\\frac{8 \\pi}{3} .\n$$\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the hypotenuse of a right triangle where both legs have a length of $\\frac{16}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}\\,\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{\\frac{16}{3}}^{2}+{\\frac{16}{3}}^{2}}=\\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s=\\frac{8 \\pi}{3}+\\frac{16 \\sqrt{2}}{3}=\\frac{8}{3}(\\pi+2\\sqrt{2}).\n$$"] [17592186564699 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d6128159e1606c72588f4881db5156ec3b73be224c160c15a0977a1eb1f824fa.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564707 :question/problem "![](images/26/26b263723ce79bb4ddb59ceac1f179447430b3cf8c3d14e3ea0dfc7612fc0665.png)\n\nCalculate ${\\int }_{C}\\,\\text{d}s,$ where $C$ is the path shown above."] [17592186564