/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2 x^{2} - 4}{y^{2}}.$ Work on an interval where $y\\ne 0.$"] [17592186564787 :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3 x^{2} - 2}{y^{2}}.$ Work on an interval where $y\\ne 0.$"] [17592186564810 :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}a & 2c \\\\ 2b & 2d\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186564813 :question/problem "Find $Mz$ if \n$M=\\begin{bmatrix}s & 2t \\\\ -2t & s\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$z=\\begin{bmatrix}s \\\\ 2t\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186564815 :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3a & 2c \\\\ b & 3d\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186564818 :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}2a & 3c \\\\ 2b & 2d\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186564839 :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}a & c \\\\ 2b & 2d\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186564840 :question/problem "Find $Ax$ if\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3a & c \\\\ b & d\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$."] [17592186565410 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[1,3].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}5({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.$"] [17592186565414 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $yz$-plane of the ellipsoid $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{12}=1?$"] [17592186565420 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=-1-t+2{t}^{2},\\,{p}_{2}(t)=t-{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3-3t.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565420 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(-1-t+2{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(t-{t}^{2}) & =3-3t \\\\ -{x}_{1}+(-{x}_{1}+{x}_{2})t+(2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =3-3t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 6\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ {x}_{2}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations, we get ${x}_{1}=-3$ and ${x}_{2}=-6.$ \n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+(-6)=-9.$"] [17592186565421 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565421 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ 0 & {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ -{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-2 \\\\ {x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=6 \\\\ {x}_{2}=4 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=4.$ From the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}=6.$\n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=6+4=10.$"] [17592186565439 :question/problem "If the solid region $R$ is given by \n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,0\\le z\\le \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\},\n$$\n\nthen ${∭}_{R}z({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}V=$"] [17592186565441 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[-8,-1].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}6Δ{y}_{j}.$"] [17592186565441 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the factor $6$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}6Δ{y}_{j}=6{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[-8,-1],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}-{y}_{0})+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})+({y}_{3}-{y}_{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}-{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}}-{y}_{0})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{y}_{3}}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}+{y}_{n} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(-1)-(-8) \\\\  & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=6\\cdot 7=42.\n$$"] [17592186565445 :question/problem "Consider the following polynomials:\n\n$$\n{p}_{1}(t)=2-t,\\,{p}_{2}(t)=3-2{t}^{2},\\,q(t)=3t-4{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{p}_{1}(t),{p}_{2}(t)\\},$ and $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565445 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=q(t)\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2-t)+{x}_{2}(3-2{t}^{2}) & =3t-4{t}^{2} \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})-{x}_{1}t-2{x}_{2}{t}^{2} & =3t-4{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $t$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{1}=3 \\\\ 3{x}_{2}=6 \\\\ -2{x}_{2}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations we get ${x}_{2}=2.$ From the  first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $[q(t)]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3+2=-1.$"] [17592186565448 :question/worked-solution "To solve the initial value problem, we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of $Y(s).$\n\nTo find the inverse transform, we first ignore the exponential term ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ (which corresponds to a time shift) and focus on the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s-3)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+16),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+16)(s-3)}=\\frac{A}{s-3}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+16}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+16)+(Bs+C)(s-3).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=3,$ we have\n\n$$\n3=A({3}^{2}+16)+0\\,⟹\\,25A=3\\,⟹\\,A=\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=-\\frac{3}{25}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=16A-3C\\,⟹\\,0=16\\,\\left(\\frac{3}{25}\\right)-3C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{16}{25}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{25}(\\frac{3}{s-3}-3\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}+4\\cdot \\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{25}(3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-3}\\}-3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}+4\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{4}{{s}^{2}+16}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3t}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/4}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}[3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $cos\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)$ and $sin\\,(4\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{25}(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{25}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})(3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)={e}^{3t}+\\frac{1}{25}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=3{e}^{3(t-\\pi /4)}+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$"] [17592186565453 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[3,8].$ If $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}.$"] [17592186565460 :question/problem "Given the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{2}=1$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [17592186565464 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{z}_{0},{z}_{1},…,{z}_{q}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[-1,5].$ If $Δ{z}_{k}={z}_{k}-{z}_{k-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}.$"] [17592186565464 :question/worked-solution "Note that ${\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k}$ is just the length of the interval $[-1,5],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{k=1}^{q}Δ{z}_{k} & =({z}_{1}-{z}_{0})+({z}_{2}-{z}_{1})+({z}_{3}-{z}_{2})+⋯+({z}_{q}-{z}_{q-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{z}_{1}}-{z}_{0})+(\\cancel{{z}_{2}}-\\cancel{{z}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{z}_{3}}-\\cancel{{z}_{2}})+⋯+({z}_{q}-\\cancel{{z}_{q-1}}) \\\\  & =-{z}_{0}+{z}_{q} \\\\  & ={z}_{q}-{z}_{0} \\\\  & =5-(-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186565466 :question/worked-solution "To find the function $g(t),$ we need to compute the inverse Laplace transform of the term containing the time delay in $Y(s).$\n\nWe focus on the term with the exponential ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ (which corresponds to the step function in the time domain) and identify the rational function part:\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}.\n$$\n\nWe'll solve this in three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Partial fraction decomposition\n\nSince the denominator contains a linear factor $(s+2)$ and an irreducible quadratic factor $({s}^{2}+9),$ the decomposition takes the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{s}{({s}^{2}+9)(s+2)}=\\frac{A}{s+2}+\\frac{Bs+C}{{s}^{2}+9}.\n$$\n\nTo find the constants, we clear the denominators:\n\n$$\ns=A({s}^{2}+9)+(Bs+C)(s+2).\n$$\n\nNow, we find the constants $A,B,$ and $C.$\n\n- Setting $s=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n-2=A((-2)^{2}+9)+0\\,⟹\\,13A=-2\\,⟹\\,A=-\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the coefficients of ${s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n0=A+B\\,⟹\\,B=-A=\\frac{2}{13}.\n$$\n\n- Comparing the constant terms, we have\n\n$$\n0=9A+2C\\,⟹\\,0=9\\,\\left(-\\frac{2}{13}\\right)+2C\\,⟹\\,C=\\frac{9}{13}.\n$$\n\nThus, we can rewrite $F(s)$ as\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{13}(-\\frac{2}{s+2}+\\frac{2s+9}{{s}^{2}+9})=\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{s+2}+2\\cdot \\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}+3\\cdot \\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}).\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Finding the Inverse of the base function\n\nWe find the inverse transform $f(t)={L}^{-1}\\{F(s)\\}$ using standard transforms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s+2}\\}+2\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}+3\\cdot {L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}+9}\\}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2t}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Applying the time-shifting property\n\nFinally, we address the ${e}^{-\\pi s/3}$ term. By the second shifting theorem, we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{-as}\\cdot F(s)=L\\{u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a)\\}.\n$$\n\nTaking the inverse Laplace transform gives\n\n$$\n{L}^{-1}\\{{e}^{-as}F(s)\\}=u(t-a)\\cdot f(t-a).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Therefore, we shift $f(t)$ by replacing every instance of $t$ with $(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})f\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}[-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3}))].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the trigonometric identities $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)$ and $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3(t-\\pi /3))=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t),$ we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot \\frac{1}{13}(-2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{13}\\,u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})(2{e}^{-(t-\\pi /2)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing this to the second term of the form given in the problem statement $y(t)=-{e}^{-2t}-\\frac{1}{13}u\\,(t-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\cdot g(t),$ we identify $g(t)$ as\n\n$$\ng(t)=2{e}^{-2(t-\\pi /3)}+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t).\n$$"] [17592186565470 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[5,14].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}-2Δ{y}_{j}.$"] [17592186565470 :question/worked-solution "First, we take the factor $(-2)$ outside the summation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}-2Δ{y}_{j}=-2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[5,14],$ because\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}-{y}_{0})+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})+({y}_{3}-{y}_{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}-{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}}-{y}_{0})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}})+(\\cancel{{y}_{3}}-\\cancel{{y}_{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}+{y}_{n} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-{y}_{0} \\\\  & =14-5 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=(-2)\\cdot 9=-18.\n$$"] [17592186565472 :question/problem "Let $P=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ be a partition of the interval $[1,5].$ If $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1},$ calculate ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}3({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.$"] [17592186565477 :question/problem "${lim}_{(x,y)\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3xy+{y}^{2}}{{x}^{3}+xy+{x}^{2}y+{y}^{2}}=$"] [17592186565478 :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\n{B}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{B}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nIf $B=\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\}$ is a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{B}_{1},{B}_{2}\\},$ and $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],$ then ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=$"] [17592186565478 :question/worked-solution "We need to find ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{B}_{1}+{x}_{2}{B}_{2}=A.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given information into the above equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 9 \\\\ -6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}0 & 6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2} \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} & 3{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding entries, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}=-5 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe equations above give a linear system with the augmented matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nRow-reducing the matrix using Gaussian elimination, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M= & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ -3 & -6 & -5 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\ ∼ & [\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{3}{2} & -1 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{9}{2} & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9{x}_{2}=8 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -\\frac{9}{2}{x}_{2}=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2}{3}.$ From the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{x}_{1}+9\\cdot \\frac{2}{3} & =8 \\\\ 6{x}_{1} & =2 \\\\ {x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $[A]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{2}{3}=1.$"] [17592186565644 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{y^2}{64} - \\frac{z^2}{25} = 1$ for $y \\ge 8$ in $yz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨ y ,\\, z ⟩ = ⟨  8\\sec{t} ,\\, 5\\tan{t}⟩,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{y}^{2}}{64}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25} & =\\frac{(8\\sec  t)^{2}}{64}-\\frac{(5\\tan  t)^{2}}{25} \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\sec^{2} t}{64}-\\frac{25\\tan^{2} t}{25} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,x) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨x,\\,8\\sec  t,\\,5\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the second component of $f$ is $8\\sec{t}.$"] [17592186565646 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{x^2}{49} - \\frac{z^2}{64} = 1$ for $x \\ge 7$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨ x ,\\, z ⟩ = ⟨  7\\sec{t} ,\\, 8\\tan{t}⟩,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{49}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{64} & =\\frac{(7\\sec  t)^{2}}{49}-\\frac{(8\\tan  t)^{2}}{64} \\\\  & =\\frac{49\\sec^{2} t}{49}-\\frac{64\\tan^{2} t}{64} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨7\\sec  t,\\,y,\\,8\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $7\\sec{t}.$"] [17592186565656 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the hyperbola $\\frac{x^2}{81} - \\frac{z^2}{16} = 1$ for $x \\ge 9$ in $xz$-plane has the parametrization\n\n$$\n⟨ x ,\\, z ⟩ = ⟨  9\\sec{t} ,\\, 4\\tan{t}⟩,\n\n\\qquad t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2},\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nsince \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{81}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{16} & =\\frac{(9\\sec  t)^{2}}{81}-\\frac{(4\\tan  t)^{2}}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{81\\sec^{2} t}{81}-\\frac{16\\tan^{2} t}{16} \\\\  & =\\sec^{2} t-\\tan^{2} t \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t,y) & =⟨x,\\,y,\\,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨9\\sec  t,\\,y,\\,4\\tan  t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $t \\in \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)$ and $y \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $9\\sec{t}.$"] [17592186566196 :multistep/context "A company produces two types of products, type A and type B. The revenue function of the company, in thousands of dollars, is given by $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1,\n$$\n\nwhere $x={[\\begin{aligned}\\,x & y\\,\\end{aligned}]}^{T},$ $x$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product A, and $y$ is the price (in thousands of dollars) per unit of product B. The company can adjust the prices per unit for both types of products by adding or removing certain features."] [17592186566203 :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xz$-plane of the elliptic paraboloid $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{8}?$"] [17592186566214 :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-2 & =0 \\\\ 4x & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\frac{1}{2})\\cup (\\frac{1}{2},\\infty ).$"] [17592186566234 :question/problem "Given the elliptic paraboloid $z+8=\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{6}$, find its intercepts with the $y$-axis."] [17592186566235 :question/worked-solution "The (population) covariance of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}$ are the means of the data sets.\n\nFirst, we need to compute the means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (4+4+(-4)+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\overset{―}{y} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot ((-1)+(-2)+7+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of our data sets is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(4-2)(-1-2)+(4-2)(-2-2)+(-4-2)(7-2)+(4-2)(4-2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[(2)(-3)+(2)(-4)+(-6)(5)+(2)(2)] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[-6-8-30+4] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (-40) \\\\  & =-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186566269 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of one sheet $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{(z-3)^{2}}{9}=1$, find its $y$-intercepts."] [17592186566270 :question/problem "Find the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186567013 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={337.5}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${337.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $4$th quadrant.\n\n![](images/a2/a248eaf8b200e2788db08164f77c2d3d457837e851726f54ca22f19cbc1dc440.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{15\\pi }{8}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =2\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{15\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{15\\pi }{8}\\right)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567017 :question/problem "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}$ . *\n\nI. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ don't intersect\n\nII. The lines $ax+by=0$ and $cx+dy=0$ intersect at infinitely many points\n\nIII. The system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution."] [17592186567017 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is true ($det(A)\\ne 0$), all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only option III gives us a true statement: \n\n*If $det[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}a & b \\\\ c & d\\end{aligned}]x=0$ has the unique zero solution.* $\\,✓$"] [17592186567023 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-y+7) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-y+7) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-xy+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-y & -x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}x{y}^{3}-y+7 \\\\ {x}^{3}-xy+1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{2}^{3} & 3(-1)(2)^{2}-1 \\\\ 3(-1)^{2}-2 & -(-1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-1)(2)^{3}-2+7 \\\\ (-1)^{3}-(-1)(2)+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8 & -13 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{21} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{21}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{44}{21} \\\\ \\frac{23}{21}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.095 \\\\ 1.095\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567024 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={132}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is equivalent to ${132}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/23/23a979aa6a346c2572ecc108e3b88ac4cbe3f192dc6818c975c9373cbca33e74.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{15}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{15}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{15}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567025 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2,7]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,4]$\n\nCalculate  ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [17592186567025 :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[0,4].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=4-0=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we can evaluate as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{n} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}-{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={7}^{2}-{2}^{2} \\\\  & =45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=45\\cdot 4=180.\n$$"] [17592186567034 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nLet $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}].$ If $k\\ne 2$, then\n\n$$\ndet(A)=det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}]=4-2k\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, according to the invertible matrix theorem $A$ is invertible and $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ is the unique solution of  $Ax=0.$\nAlso, the system \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nhas exactly one solution, and the lines $2x-y=1$ and $4x-ky=0$ intersect at exactly one point.  \n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*If $k\\ne 2$, then the system $[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has exactly one solution.* $\\,✓$"] [17592186567040 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as\n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{3}-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{3}-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 2y \\\\ {y}^{3} & 3x{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-4 \\\\ x{y}^{3}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6(1) & 2(1) \\\\ {1}^{3} & 3(1)(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}+(1)^{2}-4 \\\\ (1)(1)^{3}-2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567049 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186567055 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={150}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is equivalent to ${150}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/d0/d04b5b75e4406104bd2621d54be389e87cb9e289199aac60db449fe3244a9b45.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is in the $2$nd  quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567056 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fourth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false. If $k=2$ then\n\n$$\ndet[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }k & 2\\end{aligned}]=det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\text{ }2 & 2\\end{aligned}]=1\\cdot 2-1\\cdot 2=0,\n$$\n\nbut  according to the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem, the determinant cannot be $0.$\n\n- Statement III is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."] [17592186567057 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,4]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2,8]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [17592186567057 :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are just the lengths of the intervals $[0,4]$ and $[2,8]$ respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =4-0=4, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =8-2=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=4\\cdot 6=24.\n$$"] [17592186567058 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{5}-y+10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{5}-y+10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy-{y}^{2}+3)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}5{x}^{4} & -1 \\\\ y & x-2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{5}-y+10 \\\\ xy-{y}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5(-1)^{4} & -1 \\\\ 1 & (-1)-2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{5}-(1)+10 \\\\ (-1)(1)-{1}^{2}+3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{23}{14} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{37}{14} \\\\ \\frac{11}{14}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.643 \\\\ 0.786\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567060 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,3]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[-2,0]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}7({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [17592186567060 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n7{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ is just the length of the interval $[-2,0].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=0-(-2) & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $i,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i} & =({x}_{1}+{x}_{0})Δ{x}_{1}+({x}_{2}+{x}_{1})Δ{x}_{2}+⋯+({x}_{m}+{x}_{m-1})Δ{x}_{n} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}\\,+\\,{x}_{0})({x}_{1}\\,-\\,{x}_{0})\\,+\\,({x}_{2}\\,+\\,{x}_{1})({x}_{2}\\,-\\,{x}_{1})\\,+\\,⋯\\,+\\,({x}_{m}\\,+\\,{x}_{m-1})({x}_{m}\\,-\\,{x}_{m-1}) \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})+({x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{m-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}}-{x}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{x}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{x}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({x}_{m}^{2}-\\cancel{{x}_{m-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{0}^{2}+{x}_{m}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n7{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}({x}_{i}+{x}_{i-1})Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=7\\cdot 8\\cdot 2=112.\n$$"] [17592186567062 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,2]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[1,2]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}2Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [17592186567062 :question/worked-solution "Using the constant multiple and product rules, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}2Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} & =2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j} \\\\  & =2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that both ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ and ${\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}$ are equal to the length of the interval $[1,2].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i} & =2-1=1, \\\\ {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j} & =2-1=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nCombining our results, we have \n\n$$\n2{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{y}_{j}=2\\cdot 1\\cdot 1=2.\n$$"] [17592186567064 :question/problem "$\\int 5-{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186567067 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-3x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & 2y \\\\ -3 & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-10 \\\\ {y}^{3}-3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-2) & 2(-2) \\\\ -3 & 3(-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{2}+(-2)^{2}-10 \\\\ (-2)^{3}-3(-2)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -3 & 12\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 12-(-4)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}12 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}-32 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{38}{15} \\\\ -\\frac{59}{30}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-2.533 \\\\ -1.967\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567068 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={225}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to ${225}^{∘},$ which lies in the $3\\text{rd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d676fb0c9d75ee8230340203ec1a853e34384061ce899ddfc0185e49d2f8dbec.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)$ must be *positive* because the tangent ratio is *always* positive in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567073 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\sqrt{x}(2+x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186567074 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is false by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is true by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."] [17592186567076 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{3}-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2} & -2y \\\\ -2x & 3{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{3}-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3(-1)^{2} & -2(1) \\\\ -2(-1) & 3(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-{1}^{2}+1 \\\\ {1}^{3}-(-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{13}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{10}{13} \\\\ \\frac{11}{13}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.769 \\\\ 0.846\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567077 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nAlso, we are told that the second part of the invertible matrix theorem is true. So, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement I is true by the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement II is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, only statement I is true."] [17592186567082 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be re-written as \n\n$$\n{J}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-y+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-y+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy & {x}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-y+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4(1)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(1)(-2) & {1}^{2}-1\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-(-2)^{2}+1 \\\\ (1)^{2}(-2)-(-2)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1.25 \\\\ -0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2.25 \\\\ -2.75\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567083 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{x}^{5}+7}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186567088 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is equivalent to ${120}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd  quadrant.\n\n![](images/34/342602449228f93042121b22f39d6cc203a6d945934a9ddb726e862838832815.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ is in the $2$nd  quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567089 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={198}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${198}^{∘},$ which lies in the $3$rd quadrant.\n\n![](images/4f/4f66f8119626f97d6439aee81f5a02b1893a4dc2a787addc6c4a71ff865e3687.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\pi }{10}-\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the cosine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{10}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567092 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2x+5) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2x+5)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{3} & -2y \\\\ 2xy-2 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{y}^{2}+3 \\\\ {x}^{2}y-2x+5\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(-2)^{3} & -2(-2) \\\\ 2(-2)(-2)-2 & (-2)^{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-2)^{4}-(-2)^{2}+3 \\\\ (-2)^{2}(-2)-2(-2)+5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 6 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-32)\\cdot 4-4\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -32\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{152}[\\begin{aligned}56 \\\\ -122\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-1.632 \\\\ -2.803\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places."] [17592186567097 :question/problem "Let $R=[1,3]\\times [-1,1]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i-1}-2{y}_{j}-2)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}(4{x}_{i}-2{y}_{j-1}-2)Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following results:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0}) \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}-{x}_{0})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567099 :question/problem "Consider the following partitions:\n\n- ${P}_{1}=\\{{x}_{0},{x}_{1},…,{x}_{m}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[2,5]$\n\n- ${P}_{2}=\\{{y}_{0},{y}_{1},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is a partition of the interval $[0,3]$\n\nCalculate ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j},$ where $Δ{x}_{i}={x}_{i}-{x}_{i-1}$ and $Δ{y}_{j}={y}_{j}-{y}_{j-1}.$"] [17592186567099 :question/worked-solution "Using the product rule, we can write the summation as\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}.\n$$\n\nNote that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}$ is just the length of the interval $[2,5].$ So, we have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}=5-2=3.\n$$\n\nFor the sum over $j,$ we evaluate it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j} & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})Δ{y}_{1}+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})Δ{y}_{2}+⋯+({y}_{n}+{x}_{n-1})Δ{y}_{n} \\\\  & =({y}_{1}+{y}_{0})({y}_{1}-{y}_{0})+({y}_{2}+{y}_{1})({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})+⋯+({y}_{n}+{x}_{n-1})({y}_{n}-{y}_{n-1}) \\\\  & =({y}_{1}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2})+({y}_{2}^{2}-{y}_{1}^{2})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{n-1}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}}-{y}_{0}^{2})+(\\cancel{{y}_{2}^{2}}-\\cancel{{y}_{1}^{2}})+⋯+({y}_{n}^{2}-\\cancel{{y}_{n-1}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-{y}_{0}^{2}+{y}_{n}^{2} \\\\  & ={y}_{n}^{2}-{y}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCombining our results, we finally arrive at \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}Δ{x}_{i}\\cdot {\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({y}_{j}+{y}_{j-1})Δ{y}_{j}=3\\cdot 9=27.\n$$"] [17592186567100 :question/problem "Let \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $det(A)=0$\n\nII. The system  $Ax=0$ has exactly one solution\n\nIII. $A$ is invertible"] [17592186567100 :question/worked-solution "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n1\\. $A$ is invertible.\n\n2\\. The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n3\\. The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n4\\. The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n5\\. $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. We have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =det[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0-(-3)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the fifth part of the invertible matrix theorem is false, all the other parts of the theorem are false too. Therefore, according to the theorem:\n\n- Statement II is false by the third part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\n- Statement III is false by the first part of the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore only statements I is true."] [17592186567105 :question/problem "Let $R=[0,6]\\times [1,2]$ be a region in the $xy$-plane, and let $f(x,y)$ be a function defined over $R.$\nThe lower and upper Riemann sums corresponding to an arbitrary partition $P$ of $R$ are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}-{y}_{j})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}, \\\\ U(f,P) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-{y}_{j-1})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the definition of the double integral in terms of $L(f,P)$ and $U(f,P),$ compute ${∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the following result:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{m}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{n}({x}_{i-1}+{x}_{i})Δ{x}_{i}Δ{y}_{j}=({x}_{m}^{2}-{x}_{0}^{2})({y}_{n}-{y}_{0})\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567109 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of finding the zeros of a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{J}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${J}_{f}({x}_{n})$ is the Jacobian of $f$ evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the Jacobian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-2x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2x & -3{y}^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{y}^{3}-1 \\\\ {y}^{2}-2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(-1) & -3(1)^{2} \\\\ -2 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{2}-(1)^{3}-1 \\\\ (1)^{2}-2(-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-0.3 \\\\ 0.2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186567110 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={112.5}^{∘}\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is equivalent to ${112.5}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/9b/9b4b14f758b27c3bf9af0edec9fd6125fa268e4ec90b9138a986bb81afd48fc5.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\right)=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567111 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={144}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{4\\pi }{5}$ is equivalent to ${144}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/d9/d9a7c21097737d5b9bcfcf81f7127651bb43bae15c77b617ef6e2ef039a339e5.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{5}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{4\\pi }{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{csc}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)$ must be positive because sine (and therefore cosecant) is always positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{5}\\right)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567112 :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={135}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is equivalent to ${135}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2$nd quadrant.\n\n![](images/bb/bb29311d0e31708c7a3d9c8eb356b12531cb2e99af10a02bdf653c167346b438.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)$  must be *positive* because the sine ratio is *always* positive in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186567350 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(- 2 x)\\,i+(- 2 y + 2)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$"] [17592186567354 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(x^{2} y)\\,i+(x + 1)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(2 t)\\,i+(t + 1)\\,j$ for $t\\in[0,1].$"] [17592186567356 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int}_{C}F\\cdot\\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(5 x)\\,i+(5 y - 5)\\,j$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)})\\,i+(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)})\\,j$ for $t\\in[\\frac{\\pi}{2},2 \\pi].$"] [17592186567365 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(-5)\\left(\\frac{y+1}{x}\\,i-\\frac{2(x-1)}{y}\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+(t-1)\\,j$ for $t\\in [3,4].$"] [17592186567368 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ given that $F(x,y)=(-1)\\left((y\\operatorname{\\sin}x)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j\\right)$ and $C$ is the path  $r(t)=\\operatorname{arcsin}t\\,i+2j$ for $t\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$"] [17592186567610 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 y z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 x z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 x y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(t\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t^{3}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [17592186567619 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(x + z\\right)\\,i+\\left(- y + z\\right)\\,j+\\left(z^{2}\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- 3 t^{2}\\right)\\,i+\\left(t^{2}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,2]$."] [17592186567622 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 z\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 y\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(2 e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(3 e^{2 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(e^{- 2 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [17592186567641 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{x}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- \\frac{3 z}{y}\\right)\\,j+\\left(-3\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(e^{t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(t\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [17592186567642 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ given that $F(x,y,z)=\\left(y\\right)\\,i+\\left(x\\right)\\,j+\\left(z\\right)\\,k$ and $C$ is the path $r(t)=\\left(- e^{- 3 t}\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,j+\\left(- 2 e^{3 t}\\right)\\,k$ for $t\\in [0,1]$."] [17592186568073 :question/problem "A company produces keyboards. The revenue and cost functions, in hundreds of dollars, of this company are given by\n$R(x) = 40x - x^{2}, C(x) = 50 + 5x$,\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced by the company per month. Find the profit function $P(x)$."] [17592186568080 :question/problem "A company produces keyboards. The revenue and cost functions, in hundreds of dollars, of this company are given by\n$R(x) = 50x - 0.05x^{2}, C(x) = 20 + 5x$,\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced by the company per month. Find the profit function $P(x)$."] [17592186568091 :question/problem "A company produces memory cards. The revenue and profit functions, in thousands of dollars, of this company are given by\n$R(x) = 25x - 0.01x^{2}, P(x) = -0.01x^{2} + 20x - 10$,\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced by the company per month. Find the cost function $C(x)$."] [17592186568098 :question/problem "A company produces keyboards. The revenue and cost functions, in hundreds of dollars, of this company are given by\n$R(x) = 20x - 0.02x^{2}, C(x) = 30 + 15x$,\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced by the company per month. Find the profit function $P(x)$."] [17592186568101 :question/problem "A company produces memory cards. The revenue and profit functions, in thousands of dollars, of this company are given by\n$R(x) = 30x - x^{2}, P(x) = -x^{2} + 20x - 5$,\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced by the company per month. Find the cost function $C(x)$."] [17592186568112 :question/problem "A company manufactures milk bottles. The cost and revenue functions, in thousands of dollars, of this company are given by\n\n$C(x) = 2860 + 12x, \\qquad R(x) = 46x - 0.1x^{2}$,\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced per year. Find the number of units the company needs to produce in a year to make a profit of $20000$ dollars.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186568113 :question/problem "A company manufactures a sports nutritional supplement. The cost and revenue functions, in hundreds of dollars, of this company are given by\n\n$C(x) = 1915 + 12x, \\qquad R(x) = 34x - 0.05x^{2}$,\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced per month. Find the number of units the company needs to produce in a month to make a profit of $10000$ dollars.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186568122 :question/problem "A company manufactures a sports nutritional supplement. The cost and revenue functions, in hundreds of dollars, of this company are given by\n\n$C(x) = 19680 + 48x, \\qquad R(x) = 240x - 0.4x^{2}$,\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced per month. Find the number of units the company needs to produce in a month to make a profit of $140000$ dollars.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186568124 :question/problem "A company manufactures a sports nutritional supplement. The cost and revenue functions, in thousands of dollars, of this company are given by\n\n$C(x) = 17400 + 7x, \\qquad R(x) = 279x - 0.8x^{2}$,\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced per month. Find the number of units the company needs to produce in a month to make a profit of $5000000$ dollars.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186568129 :question/problem "A company manufactures a sports nutritional supplement. The cost and revenue functions, in thousands of dollars, of this company are given by\n\n$C(x) = 4930 + 26x, \\qquad R(x) = 74x - 0.1x^{2}$,\n\nwhere $x$ represents the number of units produced per month. Find the number of units the company needs to produce in a month to make a profit of $20000$ dollars.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question."] [17592186569965 :question/problem "Given that \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 1 \\\\ -16 & 10\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then $L=$"] [17592186569998 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}.$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then $L=$"] [17592186570015 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${45}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{45}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$"] [17592186570027 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186570034 :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x} & =11x\\cdot {5}^{x}+4\\cdot {5}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x}-11x\\cdot {5}^{x}-4\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-11x-4)\\cdot {5}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $3{x}^{2}-11x-4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{1}{3},4,$ and their product is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\cdot 4=-\\frac{4}{3}.$"] [17592186570747 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5{x}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-10x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186570750 :question/problem "Find the derivative of $y=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}.$"] [17592186570750 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{3}+{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186570758 :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Applying this formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186571376 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 2 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186571383 :question/problem "$\\frac{{m}^{3}}{2n}\\cdot \\frac{4{n}^{2}}{{m}^{2}}=$"] [17592186571391 :question/problem "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186571396 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 7 & 2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 & -1\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186571404 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$T=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 3 & -25\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186571408 :question/problem "Find the cofactor of ${x}_{32}$ in \n$X=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 6 & -2 & 8 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186571413 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 2 & 4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 2 & -8\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186571421 :question/problem "Find the minor of  ${e}_{15}$ in  $E=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 0 & -3 & 8 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 11 & 5 & 4 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -6 & 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186571435 :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{m}{{n}^{2}m+2{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{2m+4}{m}?$"] [17592186571440 :question/problem "$\\begin{vmatrix}5 & 2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}=$"] [17592186571443 :question/problem "Calculate the determinant of the matrix\n$W=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186571448 :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $A$ is upper triangular\n\nII. $A$ is lower triangular\n\nIII. ${A}^{T}$ is lower triangular"] [17592186571454 :question/problem "Calculate\n$\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}.$"] [17592186572115 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\frac{xy}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186572117 :question/problem "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ \\sqrt{6} & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 0 & \\sqrt{5} & 2 \\\\ \\sqrt{3} & 0 & 0 & \\sqrt{5}\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [17592186572145 :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y),$ calculate $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}$ given that $\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2x+{y}^{3}}}.$"] [17592186572154 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $8\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n8\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{16},\\frac{\\pi }{16}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /16}^{\\pi /16}{cos}^{2}⁡(8\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186572159 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{3n+2}{6}.$"] [17592186572165 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\sum }_{a=1}^{3}a{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{a+1}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186572188 :question/worked-solution "The area bounded by a polar curve is given by\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{r}^{2}(\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta ))}^{2}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the integration limits. \n\nThe curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin. Therefore, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that the first petal lies between the first and fourth quadrants. So it's convenient to assume that $\\theta$ can be negative.\n\nWe now solve the above equation for $10\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n10\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the first petal is traversed for $\\theta \\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{20},\\frac{\\pi }{20}].$ Notice that this interval lies between the first and fourth quadrants, the same as our petal.\n\nTherefore, our expression for the area is given by\n\n$$\n2{\\int }_{-\\pi /20}^{\\pi /20}{cos}^{2}⁡(10\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"] [17592186572670 :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{x}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x}{{2}^{x}}\\cdot ($\n{{field-1}}\n$).$"] [17592186572695 :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ So, we first draw the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/b8/b8db5015e22e55282e180b1d81f7a2e1ca544dfe0f468dc4dedb6283bac2da5c.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $\\frac{4}{3}a.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{4}|b|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $b$ have the same direction\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\lambda b=\\frac{4}{3}a+\\frac{1}{4}b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $b,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186573250 :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to \\,4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{4}\\right)+\\pi )=$"] [17592186573257 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-2)=1,\\,{f}^{′}(-2)=-3,\\,g(-2)=2,\\,{g}^{′}(-2)=6\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=-2.$"] [17592186573271 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$"] [17592186573279 :question/problem "Given that $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -6 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 6 & 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(A).$"] [17592186573280 :question/problem "The values of functions $q(x)$ and $r(x)$ and their derivatives at $x=2$ are given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $q(x)$ | $r(x)$ | ${q}^{′}(x)$ | ${r}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $-1$ | $-3$ | $2$ |\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))$ at $x=2.$\n\n${\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3q(x)+4r(x))|}_{x=2}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186573282 :question/problem "For the functions $f(x)$ and $g(x),$ you are given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(-4)=2e,\\,{f}^{′}(-4)=-e,\\,g(-4)=-3e,\\,{g}^{′}(-4)=e\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\\right)$ at $x=-4.$"] [17592186573290 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186573296 :question/problem "Given that $D=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -7 & -1\\end{bmatrix},$ find $det(D).$"] [17592186573309 :question/problem "What is the value of ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)?$"] [17592186573309 :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right):$ \n\n![](images/ff/fffe2ba1124b8edd04902db5ad2b0945e5e04372ae0a0ed338c893f0ae417c58.png)\n\nThe domain of $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)$ is $x\\in [2,6].$ This means that $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)$ is undefined outside this interval.\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\infty }2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-4}{2}\\right)$ is undefined."] [17592186573873 :question/problem "$\\int ({4}^{x}-{e}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186573895 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\pi }{2}{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186573896 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{3x}.$"] [17592186573913 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the level surfaces for the function $f(x,y,z)$ are given by the equation\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=c.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we're interested in $c=12.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =c \\\\ 12{z}^{2}-3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}-{z}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the level surface for $c=12$ is the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{6}-{z}^{2}=-1.$"] [17592186573944 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186575547 :question/problem "$\\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-5{sec}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186575581 :question/problem "What is the value of $q(t)=\\sqrt{t-3}\\,i+ln\\,({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{5}{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)\\,k$ at $t=4?$\n\n$q(4)=$\n{{field-1}} $i\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-2}} $j\\,+\\,$\n\n{{field-3}} $k$"] [17592186575586 :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{csc}⁡x(6\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186575596 :question/problem "$\\int (8\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186575600 :question/problem "What is the value of $g(t)=⟨{3}^{\\,t-1},\\,{log}_{3}⁡({t}^{2}+2t),\\,{sec}^{2}\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)-1⟩$ at $t=1?$\n\n$g(1)=⟨$\n{{field-1}} $,\\,$\n{{field-2}} $,\\,$\n{{field-3}} $⟩$"] [17592186575615 :question/problem "Given that $r(t)=⟨cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,\\frac{4}{t-1}⟩,$ what is $|r(3)|?$\n\n$|r(3)|=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186575620 :question/problem "What is the value of $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\end{bmatrix}$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}?$"] [17592186575621 :question/problem "$\\int 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186576016 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{r^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{r^{2} - k^{2}}} + 3$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186576019 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} + 2$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186576033 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} + 11$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186576035 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} + 12$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186576037 :question/problem "Consider the equation\n$\\frac{y^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} = \\frac{k^{2}}{\\sqrt{y^{2} - k^{2}}} + 6$,\nwhere $k$ is a positive constant. Which of the following is one of the solutions to this equation?"] [17592186576163 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X=\\frac{\\log_b X}{\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=2$ to base $b=16$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{2} x=\\frac{\\log_{16} x}{\\log_{16} 2}.\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\log_{16} x}{\\log_{16} 2} &=\\frac{\\log_{16} x}{\\log_{16}\\left(16^{\\frac{1}{4}}\\right)} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\log_{16} x}{\\frac{1}{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\log_{2} x=\\frac{\\log_{16} x}{\\frac{1}{4}}$. Hence, $k=\\frac{1}{4}$."] [17592186576168 :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n\\log_a X=\\frac{\\log_b X}{\\log_b a}\n$$\n\nWe want to change from base $a=9$ to base $b=729$. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\log_{9} x=\\frac{\\log_{729} x}{\\log_{729} 9}.\n$$\n\nRewriting the expression in the denominator using exponents, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\log_{729} x}{\\log_{729} 9} &=\\frac{\\log_{729} x}{\\log_{729}\\left(729^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\right)} \\\\ &=\\frac{\\log_{729} x}{\\frac{1}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\log_{9} x=\\frac{\\log_{729} x}{\\frac{1}{3}}$. Hence, $k=\\frac{1}{3}$."] [17592186576420 :question/problem "What is $\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\times \\frac{5}{7}\\right)^{4}$?"] [17592186576425 :question/problem "What is $\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{5}$?"] [17592186576428 :question/problem "What is $\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\times \\frac{6}{7}\\right)^{4}$?"] [17592186576429 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{3}{8}\\times \\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{6}=$"] [17592186577586 :question/problem "What is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{10} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{3}$?"] [17592186577592 :question/problem "What is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{10} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{8}$?"] [17592186577594 :question/problem "What is $\\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{10} \\div \\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)^{2}$?"] [17592186577600 :question/problem "$\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{10}÷\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}\\right)^{2}=$"] [17592186577613 :question/problem "What is ${\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\div {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}$?"] [17592186578054 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^{-1} = \\frac{7}{8}$ can be expressed as"] [17592186578054 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$.\n\nIf we compare $\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right)^{-1} = \\frac{7}{8}$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=\\frac{7}{8}$, $b=\\frac{8}{7}$, and $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\log_{8/7}\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right) = -1$."] [17592186578055 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{-2} = \\frac{4}{9}$ can be expressed as"] [17592186578055 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$.\n\nIf we compare $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^{-2} = \\frac{4}{9}$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=\\frac{4}{9}$, $b=\\frac{3}{2}$, and $x=-2$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\log_{3/2}\\left(\\frac{4}{9}\\right) = -2$."] [17592186578064 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)^{-1} = \\frac{7}{4}$ can be expressed as"] [17592186578064 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a=b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b(a)=x$.\n\nIf we compare $\\left(\\frac{4}{7}\\right)^{-1} = \\frac{7}{4}$ with $a=b^x$, we get $a=\\frac{7}{4}$, $b=\\frac{4}{7}$, and $x=-1$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\log_{4/7}\\left(\\frac{7}{4}\\right) = -1$."] [17592186578106 :question/problem "$\\left(\\frac{1}{18}\\right)^{1} = \\frac{1}{18}$ is equivalent to"] [17592186578106 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a = b^x$ is equivalent to $\\log_b{a} = x$\n\nIf we compare $\\left(\\frac{1}{18}\\right)^{1} = \\frac{1}{18}$ with $a = b^x,$ we get $a = \\frac{1}{18}, b = \\frac{1}{18},$ and $x = 1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation $\\left(\\frac{1}{18}\\right)^{1} = \\frac{1}{18}$ is equivalent to $\\log_{\\frac{1}{18}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{18}\\right)} = 1.$\n\nNote that $\\log_n{\\left(n\\right)} = 1$ for any $n > 0, n \\ne 1.$"] [17592186578795 :question/worked-solution "![](images/57/574143239dacf6a0eb11307826c62db531384aa03b9c0f4bade4d77c66499c23.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{4}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{16}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{3}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{3}{5}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)}=-\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$"] [17592186578796 :question/problem "$\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186578800 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{9\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{5\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nThe angle ${90}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{9\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$"] [17592186578803 :question/worked-solution "![](images/1c/1cb6ef37aaaf228273ee44de5c6fb07a59f4e6f334702b2be1425b5988705855.png)\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{8}{9}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{64}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{17}{81}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta$ is negative in the fourth quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{9}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{8}{9}\\right)}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{8}.\n$$"] [17592186578806 :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. \n\nIn part 2 we found that $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =x\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}+f\\frac{\\text{d}(x)}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =x\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(2-3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(3+2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=0$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & =(2-3(0))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+(3+2(0))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $3.$"] [17592186578812 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=$"] [17592186578812 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\sqrt{2}+1.\n$$"] [17592186578821 :question/problem "$\\int (g(x)+\\sqrt{h(x)})\\,\\text{d}x\\,=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant."] [17592186578842 :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\right)\\,=${{field-1}}"] [17592186578849 :question/problem "Given that $f(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 5{cos}^{2}⁡t \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+7\\end{bmatrix},$ what is ${f}^{″}(0)?$\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{″}(0)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |\n|  |  | {{field-3}} |  |"] [17592186578850 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{11\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{11\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{2} \\\\ \\frac{7\\pi }{2}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nThe angle $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)$ corresponds to the point $(0,-1).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.$"] [17592186578851 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle ${315}^{∘}.$  From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${315}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$ Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{315}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$ \n\nNow, let's consider the angle ${150}^{∘}.$  From the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡({315}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} & ={\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}+\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-\\sqrt{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186578860 :question/problem "If $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}$, then $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$"] [17592186578860 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nLet's first find the value of angle $2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ where $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n$$\n2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2}=2\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$ Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }$ and $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we combine the results:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\sqrt{3}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}=\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"] [17592186578861 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $2\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n2\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$\n\n![](images/6b/6b70070505112ec0f12749b64937e3e0a8e8f72d77bfc1966a3edfb4c03c16b5.png)\n\nSince there are $4$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =4\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =9{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =9[(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))] \\\\  & =9(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186578874 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2\\pi  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right).$\n\nThe angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$"] [17592186578876 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=3$, we have ${\\omega }_{3}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{3}=\\omega ,$ the $n=3$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=3$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x)=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{3}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))+x(-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″} & =-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{3}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{3}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right):$\n\n$$\n[-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}]+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{3}}=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-3\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)+3\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right)=\\frac{8}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3\\pi A &=\\frac{8}{9} \\\\ 3\\pi B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}A &=-\\frac{8}{27\\pi } \\\\ B &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{3}}=-\\frac{8x}{27\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi x}{2}\\right).\n$$"] [17592186578883 :question/worked-solution "First, we want to find the interval for $\\theta$ that corresponds to the first petal. Since the curve bounding the region crosses itself at the origin, we want to find the values of $\\theta$ for which $r=0$, or \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us two solutions for $3\\theta \\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n3\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\,⟹\\,\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSo, the first petal is traversed for $-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\n![](images/75/75e6fabf01bb0a73e17efee8d9d52d7c1e66cfd78eccd3d5c4d1f9e087335c1f.png)\n\nSince there are $3$ petals, the total area bounded by the polar curve can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{total}} & =3\\cdot {A}_{\\text{petal}} \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6\\theta ))\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{(\\theta +\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6\\theta ))|}_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}[(\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi ))-(-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi ))) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186579506 :question/problem "Evaluate the repeated integral \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$"] [17592186579529 :question/problem "Identify the surface $\\frac{{z}^{2}}{9}=9{x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}.$"] [17592186579537 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with ${945}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{945}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={585}^{∘} \\\\ {585}^{∘}-{360}^{∘} & ={225}^{∘}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ca/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${225}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({945}^{∘})=4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-2\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"] [17592186579538 :question/problem "Which of the following equalities are true given that\n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\}.$\n\nI. ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x$$\\,$\n\nII. ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$\n\nIII. ${∬}_{D}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}\\,\\text{d}A={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)+2}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y$"] [17592186579540 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}\\frac{y}{x}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$"] [17592186579543 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{300}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{300}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={60}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{300}^{∘})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}.$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${60}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [17592186579551 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}9{x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A,$ where \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 2,\\,-2\\le y\\le -1\\}.$"] [17592186579566 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x:$\n\n- The minimum value is $-1,$ and the maximum value is $1.$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Its zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$\n\n- Its maximum values occur at $0,\\pm 2\\pi ,…$\n\n- Its minimum values occur at $\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 3\\pi ,…$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)"] [17592186579568 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+2\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(-\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\sqrt{2}.$"] [17592186579570 :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $-{210}^{∘}$ that lies in the range $[0,{360}^{∘}).$\n\n$$\n-{210}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={150}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡(-{210}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘}).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${150}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{150}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({150}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({150}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({150}^{∘})}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"] [17592186579576 :question/problem "Evaluate \n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{\\pi }^{7\\pi /6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.$"] [17592186579637 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} \\frac{1}{343} = 3$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{343}$, $b = \\frac{1}{7}$, and $x = 3$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{3} = \\frac{1}{343}$. Consequently, $a = \\frac{1}{343}$."] [17592186579644 :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} \\frac{1}{49} = 2$ is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{x} = \\frac{1}{49}$. What is the value of $x$?"] [17592186579644 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{7}} \\frac{1}{49} = 2$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{49}$, $b = \\frac{1}{7}$, and $x = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{7}\\right)^{2} = \\frac{1}{49}$. Consequently, $x = 2$."] [17592186579647 :question/problem "The equation $\\log_{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{32} = 5$ is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{x} = \\frac{1}{32}$. Determine the value of $x$."] [17592186579647 :question/worked-solution "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b} a = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{\\frac{1}{2}} \\frac{1}{32} = 5$ with $\\log_{b} a = x$, we get $a = \\frac{1}{32}$, $b = \\frac{1}{2}$, and $x = 5$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^{5} = \\frac{1}{32}$. Consequently, $x = 5$."] [17592186580226 :question/problem "$\\int -\\frac{2}{x\\sqrt{3(1-{x}^{2})}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580230 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int 5{coth}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580232 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,+\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37725a42df9b709dbef121957bc5d177e50ebb682e6bc97bbff8373768f7be11.png)"] [17592186580234 :question/worked-solution "The angle ${180}^{∘}$ is a quadrantal angle. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/79/792a778286533d589a3410223aaf197b07e64ea77566042d7bb8ac13fbad9768.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle ${180}^{∘}$ corresponds to the point $(-1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})=-1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘})=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡({180}^{∘})=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡({180}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡({180}^{∘})}=\\frac{-1}{0}.\n$$\n\nHowever, division by zero is undefined. Therefore, $\\operatorname{cot}⁡({180}^{∘})$ is undefined."] [17592186580236 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,+\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37725a42df9b709dbef121957bc5d177e50ebb682e6bc97bbff8373768f7be11.png)"] [17592186580243 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain is all real numbers except at the integer multiples of $\\pi$: $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n\n- The zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points that are not in the domain: $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…,$ and at each asymptote, the graph tends to $-\\infty$ from the left and $\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/1d/1d0510d5dc22d3ecb81f4cbaa67789c84b1fdc1a40065c0d2527683271e261a6.png)"] [17592186580244 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer. \n\nI. The function has zeros at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\nII. The function has minimum values at $x=…-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{7\\pi }{2}...$\n\nIII. The range of the function is $y\\in [-1,1].$"] [17592186580245 :question/problem "${\\int }_{3}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}({5}^{y-3}\\sqrt{x})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y=$"] [17592186580247 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{\\sqrt{x-{x}^{3}}-1}{2x\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580250 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{(2-2{x}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$"] [17592186580251 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nThe domain of the function contains all real numbers except for $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\nOf the given values, the only one that is **not** in the domain is  $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{2}.$"] [17592186580255 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/e0/e01402141f79b247b05980f7f4e830c112a7dcdc24232a8a4c9beefc1de6f603.png)\n\nNote that the zeros of the graph occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$\n\nOf the given options, the only one that is **not** a zero of the function is $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.$"] [17592186580258 :question/problem "Evaluate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{2}\\sqrt{{(4x+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580259 :question/problem "Calculate the definite integral ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2{x}^{3}+4}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580260 :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{2}{v-7}+\\frac{5}{v-1}=\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}-8v+7}.$"] [17592186580272 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{3}{2+2{sinh}^{2}⁡(1-3x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580273 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n\n- The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$ At each asymptote, the graph tends to $\\infty$ from the left and $-\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)"] [17592186580281 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{4}\\frac{1-{csch}^{4}⁡x}{{csch}^{2}⁡x\\,{coth}^{2}⁡x-{coth}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580284 :question/problem "Evaluate the double integral ${∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A,$ where \n$D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2\\}.$"] [17592186580285 :question/problem "The functions $f(x)$ and $g(x)$ are twice-differentiable on an open interval containing $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ The values of $f,$ $g,$ and their respective derivatives are given below:\n\n| $x$ | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | ${f}^{″}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ | ${g}^{″}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ | $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $0$ | $0$ | $-2$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)}.$"] [17592186580285 :question/worked-solution "Notice that if we try to substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)} & ={\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{g\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we use L'Hopital's rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x))}^{′}}{(g(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ again, but once more, we reach the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{f}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{{g}^{′}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe apply L'Hopital's rule again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x)}{{g}^{′}(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{′}(x))}^{′}}{({g}^{′}(x))^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand this time, when we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we are actually able to determine the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)} & ={\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-{f}^{″}(x)}{{g}^{″}(x)}|}_{x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-{f}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{{g}^{″}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 1-4}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to (\\pi /2)}\\frac{\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)-f(x)}{g(x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)}=\\frac{3}{2}.$"] [17592186580293 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,+\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $2\\pi .$\n\n- It has no zeros and no global extrema.\n\n- The branches of the function alternate between positive and negative, with a positive branch in $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$\n\nOf the given functions, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/37/37725a42df9b709dbef121957bc5d177e50ebb682e6bc97bbff8373768f7be11.png)"] [17592186580299 :question/worked-solution "The angles $0$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are quadrantal angles. Let's draw out the quadrantal angles on the unit circle.\n\n![](images/ed/edad8c833f65c54453fe40a326cbf9394c263213934447872b530a8e4fb86de1.png)\n\nLet's first consider the angle $0.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $0$ corresponds to the point $(1,0).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡0=1.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡0=0.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ From the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ corresponds to the point $(0,1).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=1.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}=\\frac{0}{1}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡0+\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=0+0=0.\n$$"] [17592186580304 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\pi -\\pi {x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x$ for $|x|>1.$"] [17592186580308 :question/problem "Using the cumulative distribution function table of the Poisson random variable $X∼\\text{Po}(15)$ given below, compute $P(X=9).$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ | $8$ | $9$ | $10$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $P(X\\le x)$ | $\\,0.0028\\,$ | $\\,0.0076\\,$ | $\\,0.0180\\,$ | $\\,0.0374\\,$ | $\\,0.0699\\,$ | $\\,0.1185\\,$ | $\\,0.1848\\,$ |\n\n$P(X=9)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580310 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)\n\nThe domain of the function contains all real numbers except for $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$ \n\nOf the given values, the only one that lies in the domain is $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.$"] [17592186580314 :question/problem "Evaluate the definite integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(3x+1)}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580315 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}({coth}^{2}⁡x-{coth}^{2}⁡x{tanh}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580316 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall some of the basic properties of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n- The domain of the function is almost $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ except for the points where the function is undefined, namely $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…$\n\n- The range of the function is $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The period of the function is $\\pi .$\n\n- There is no minimum or maximum value.\n\n- The zeros occur at $x=0,\\pm \\pi ,\\pm 2\\pi ,…$\n\n- The graph has vertical asymptotes at the points not in the domain: $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},….$ At each asymptote, the graph tends to $\\infty$ from the left and $-\\infty$ from the right.\n\nOf the given plots, the only one that has all of these properties is as follows:\n\n![](images/32/326418398ce1a33f03a21f3bdb58107856f4ccb72241429f1604486281e60822.png)"] [17592186580321 :question/problem "${\\int }_{\\pi /4}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580323 :question/problem "Compute the exact value of the definite integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{3}}\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580879 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡({x}^{3})\\operatorname{tan}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580882 :question/problem "Calculate the integral ${\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cot}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580887 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 3{x}^{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡({x}^{3})\\operatorname{cot}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580890 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /12}^{\\pi /9}6\\operatorname{sec}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580891 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /10}4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580892 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},....$\n\nII. $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ has a minimum value at $x=\\frac{7\\pi }{2}$, which is equal to $-1.$\n\nIII. The range of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ is $[-1,1].$"] [17592186580893 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}}}\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580897 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡(x)}{2+3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580904 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x)}{2+\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x)}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580915 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1/2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580917 :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x}{1+{tan}^{3}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x$"] [17592186580918 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580921 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580925 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{\\pi /18}^{\\pi /9}3{sec}^{2}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580927 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\operatorname{tan}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580930 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{\\pi /4}}8x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186580932 :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nLet's now sketch the phase portrait.\n\n- First, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are the equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=-x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/29/29e69d53d09adec27ffc6dee8eb1c343337d0a5c14bedeaaccda5ffb5d6c81bf.png)\n\n- Now, the line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$ has the equation \n$y=4x$\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{-5t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on that line. Since ${e}^{-5t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach the origin as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n![](images/1b/1babfed2e0d3b7d1c461fb743937afe5d3740b30acae2b165bda1d557d87079b.png)\n\nAll other non-equilibrium solutions are parallel to the vector ${v}_{2}.$ So, the phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/6d/6d78796b92816a9c78ca0f039cbe476116cfc4ba1a0c88380fbbb93fe4b2d49f.png)"] [17592186580935 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{x}{3}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580944 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186580945 :question/worked-solution "A function $f:X\\to Y$ is a **surjection** (also known as **onto**) if, for *every* element $y$ in $Y,$ there is at least one element in $X$ that maps to $y.$ \n\nSymbolically, we can write:\n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each function.\n\n- I is *not*  a surjection.  There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=0.$ In fact, ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $\\in R.$\n\n- II is *not* a surjection. As a counterexample, choose $2\\in Z.$ There is no number $x\\in Z$ such that $g(x)=2.$ The only number $x$ that satisfies $g(x)={x}^{3}=2$ is $x=\\sqrt[3]{2}\\notin Z.$\n\n- III is a surjection. Given $y\\in Q,$ choose $x=-\\frac{y}{2}\\in Q.$ Then $h(x)=h\\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)=-2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{y}{2}\\right)=y.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."] [17592186580948 :question/problem "${\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{16+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580949 :question/problem "$\\int 5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186580956 :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n![](images/04/04f13f2dec860874174f51990f4260615e7be893a6be5f86b3b13857c4e8953f.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function reaches its minimum value at $x=\\pi \\pm 2\\pi ,\\pi \\pm 4\\pi ,…,$ which can be written as $x=\\pi +2n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement II is true. The zeros occur at $x=\\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pm \\frac{3\\pi }{2},…,$ which can be written as $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The minimum value is $-1$, the maximum value is $1$, and so the range is $[-1,1].$"] [17592186581646 :question/worked-solution "Recall that: \n\n- the product of two rational numbers is *always* rational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{rational}=\\text{rational}\n$$\n\n- the product of a nonzero rational number and an irrational number is *always* irrational:\n\n$$\n\\text{rational}\\times \\text{irrational}=\\text{irrational}\n$$\n\n- the product of two irrational numbers is *sometimes* rational and *sometimes* irrational.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements.\n\n- Expression I is irrational. Since ${10}^{12}$ is rational and $\\sqrt{5}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n\n- Expression II is irrational. We have $\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{{13}^{5}}=\\frac{1}{{13}^{5}}\\cdot \\sqrt{6},$ and since $\\frac{1}{{13}^{5}}$ is rational and $\\sqrt{6}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n\n- Expression III is irrational. Since $3.777$ is rational and $\\sqrt{31}$ is irrational, their product must be irrational.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186581656 :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186581662 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{{e}^{5x+2}}{5}\\,\\text{d}x=$"] [17592186581666 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{{e}^{3}}\\frac{3{ln}^{2}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186581678 :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}=1.\n$$"] [17592186581694 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}2\\cdot {3}^{6-3x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186581695 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x?$"] [17592186581701 :question/problem "Given that $A=3$ and $B=2,$\n\n$\\int \\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$+\\,C.$"] [17592186581715 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{3}^{9}\\frac{4{log}_{3}⁡({x}^{2})}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$"] [17592186581718 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=${{field-1}}\n$+\\,C$"] [17592186581720 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int 8\\cdot {2}^{4x+6}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186581722 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}3{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x=$  \n{{field-1}}"] [17592186581732 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{1}{2}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"] [17592186581740 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{6}{x{ln}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$"]}
